PATCH 189: fix the 22dd script-interpreter cursor RE-SEED -- d549 is read as a BYTE, not a 16-bit word.

The in-mission command/script interpreter FUN_0000_22dd dispatches handlers through a cursor
(DAT_2000_0a86, the patch-185 uint16 DGROUP near-offset).  Each handler's tail RE-SEEDS the cursor
for the next script phase from a d549-indexed pointer table.  asm 0x23b0-0x23bd (shared by the tails
of FUN_0000_2322 and its mid-entry siblings FUN_0000_2380 / FUN_0000_2387):

    xor bx,bx            ; bx = 0
    mov bl,[0x1549]      ; bx = BYTE d549  (zero-extended -- ONLY the low byte)
    mov ax,[0x4a88+bx]   ; ax = word[DGROUP:0x4a88 + d549]   (the next handler-offset sub-table)
    mov [0x4a86],ax      ; cursor = ax

d549 is a BYTE.  Ghidra (and patch 186's reconstruction of the 2322 tail) read DAT_1000_d549 as the
16-bit WORD at DGROUP:0x1549.  But the adjacent byte d54a (DGROUP:0x154a) is NONZERO on the mission
path -- FUN_1000_67e3 sets d54a = byte[0x23b19] (observed d54a = 0x02) -- so the 16-bit read yields
0x021e instead of 0x1e, and word[0x4a88 + 0x021e] = word[0x4ca6] = 0x0000.  The cursor is then set to
0, the 22dd loop dispatches word[DGROUP:0] = 0 (an unresolved near target that traps to 0), the cursor
never advances, and the interpreter spins forever inside e714 -> 459a -> 22dd (the "op-0x4c frame
loop" hang -- the extender op-0x4c posts are the async timer-pump present, NOT the primary blocker).

Fix (asm-verified, base-loss/width class -- same idiom as patch 119's byte/word table fixes): read
d549 as a BYTE and index the DGROUP table at 0x4a88 explicitly.  Applied at all three re-seed sites:
the patch-186 2322 tail, and the two pristine mid-entry siblings FUN_0000_2380 / FUN_0000_2387 (which
share the identical 0x23b0-0x23bd tail asm and carried the same Ghidra 16-bit + &0a88-host-ptr form).

VALIDATED (FIST_CASCADE=1 + the patch-172 3-click route, from repo root): pre-189 the 22dd loop
dispatches handler 0x2322 ONCE then spins on cursor=0/tgt=0 forever (trace: 199x `cur=0000 tgt=0000`).
Post-189 the cursor correctly advances 0x4aae -> word[0x4a88+0x1e]=0x6c8e and the loop dispatches the
NEXT handler (tgt=0x23ea).  The new frontier is that handler: 0x23ea (+ its mid-entry siblings 0x23e5
/0x23ef) is an UNPROMOTED engine mid-function entry (`mov bx,0x6c34; jmp 0x23f2` -- a fixed-point
coordinate/scaling handler) that Ghidra never recovered, so it traps; recovering + reconstructing it
is the next step toward populating the viewport geometry.

CASCADE-ONLY / BEHAVIOUR-NEUTRAL: the 22dd interpreter is gated by byte[DGROUP:0x1548] (d548) > 0,
which is 0 on the menu and every one of the 19 verified flows (the re-seed is never reached there).
default boot rc=0, mainmenu md5 unchanged, verify.sh both = 19/19.

